{"cuisine/honduran-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/honduran-cuisine","n":"Honduran cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Honduras","intro":[["Honduran cuisine is a fusion of Mesoamerican, Spanish, Caribbean and African cuisines. Coconut and coconut milk are featured in both sweet and savory dishes. Regional specialties include sopa de caracol, fried fish, tamales, carne asada and baleadas. Other commonly served dishes include meat roasted with chismol and carne asada, chicken with rice and corn, and fried fish with pickled onions and jalapeños. In the coastal areas and the Bay Islands, seafood and some meats are prepared in many ways, including with coconut milk."],["Among the soups the Hondurans prepare and eat often are bean soup, mondongo soup (tripe soup), seafood soups, and beef soups. Generally all of these soups are mixed with plantains, yuca, and cabbage, and served with corn tortillas."],["Other typical dishes are montucas or corn tamales, stuffed tortillas, and tamales wrapped in plantain leaves. Typical Honduran dishes also include a wide variety of tropical fruits such as papaya, pineapple, plum, sapote, passion fruit, and bananas, which are prepared in many ways while they are still green."],["Common beverages served with dinner or lunch include soft drinks, agua de nance and horchata. A well-known domestic bottled soft drink is banana-flavored Tropical Banana."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"honduran-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honduran_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"More from Honduras","count":1,"links":[["Garifuna foodways","cuisine/garifuna-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/2c/Sopa_de_Caracol.jpg/500px-Sopa_de_Caracol.jpg","w":500,"h":282,"by":"Jislorimers","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sopa_de_Caracol.jpg"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-turks-and-caicos-islands":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-turks-and-caicos-islands","n":"Turks and Caicos cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Turks and Caicos Is.","intro":[["The Turks and Caicos Islands consist of 30 islands situated in the Atlantic Ocean, southeast of the Bahamas. Local cuisine employs local seafood, bananas, citrus, corn and ocean salt. Because the territories are situated in the Caribbean zone and belong to the United Kingdom, many elements from these cultures can be found in the local fusion cuisine."],["The variety of meat ranges from fish, fish roe, crabs, and lobster, to poultry, pork and beef. Meat is typically served with local corn, rice and vegetables and is topped with local sauces and spices."],["A traditional Turks and Caicos meal comprises grits, which are made with dried conch or peas, various local vegetables, and chicken or fish. Conch is present in a large number of dishes including conch salad, conch fritters, and cracked conch. Rum is an important drink and flavoring for cakes and even steaks. A rum-based punch is served during celebrations, and is a popular drink in the coastal bars and eateries."],["These islands host approximately 50,000 tourists per year, so the local cuisine has to accommodate the tourist trade. In Cockburn Town, cafes and open bars on the coast produce fish and seafood dishes, as well as exotic desserts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-the-turks-and-caicos-islands"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_the_Turks_and_Caicos_Islands"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/east-india-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/east-india-cuisine","n":"East Indian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["East India is a region consisting of the Indian states of Bihar, Jharkhand, Odisha"],["and West Bengal and also the union territory of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands. Chhattisgarh is sometimes included, due to its geographical continuity as well as cultural and economic integration with the Eastern region."],["The states of Bihar and West Bengal lie on the Indo-Gangetic Plain. Jharkhand is situated on the Chota Nagpur Plateau. Odisha lies on the Eastern Ghats and the Deccan Plateau. West Bengal's capital Kolkata is the largest city of this region. The Kolkata Metropolitan Area is the country's third largest metropolitan region. The region is bounded by Bhutan, Nepal and the state of Sikkim in the north, the states of Uttar Pradesh and Chhattisgarh on the west, the state of Andhra Pradesh in the south and the country of Bangladesh in the east. It is also bounded by the Bay of Bengal in the south-east. It is connected to the Seven Sister States of Northeast India by the narrow Siliguri Corridor in the north east of West Bengal. East India has the fourth-largest gross domestic product of all Indian regions."],["The region was ruled by several empires, including Gangaridai, Nandas, Mauryans, Guptas, Palas, Bhauma-Kara dynasty, Senas, Eastern Gangas, Gajapatis, Delhi Sultanate, Bengal Sultanate, Mughal Empire, and the British Empire."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"east-india-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_India#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within East Indian cooking","count":5,"links":[["Bengali cuisine","cuisine/bengali-cuisine"],["Bihari cuisine","cuisine/bihari-cuisine"],["Jharkhandi cuisine","cuisine/jharkhandi-cuisine"],["Odia cuisine","cuisine/odia-cuisine"],["Oriya cuisine","cuisine/oriya-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/dc/Lingaraj_Temple_complex_at_dusk_during_Shivratri_2020.jpg/500px-Lingaraj_Temple_complex_at_dusk_during_Shivratri_2020.jpg","w":500,"h":240,"by":"Government of Odisha","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lingaraj_Temple_complex_at_dusk_during_Shivratri_2020.jpg"}},"cuisine/batak-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/batak-cuisine","n":"Batak cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Batak cuisine is the cuisine and cooking traditions of Batak ethnic groups, predominantly found in Northern Sumatra region, Indonesia. Batak cuisine is part of Indonesian cuisine, and compared to other Sumatran cuisine traditions, it is more indigenously preserved. One characteristic of Batak cuisine is its preference to andaliman (Zanthoxylum acanthopodium) as the main spice. That is why andaliman in Indonesia sometimes dubbed as \"Batak pepper\" (Indonesian: Merica Batak)."],["Batak people are majority Christian — unlike neighboring Muslim-majority ethnic groups such as Aceh and Minang — Christian Batak people are not restricted to Islamic halal dietary law. Many of the Batak's popular meals are made of pork as well as dishes made from unusual ingredients, such as dog meat or blood, however there are also halal batak dishes, mostly chicken, beef, lamb, mutton, and freshwater fishes."],["Batak culinary centers are located in towns of Batak highlands, such as the town of Kabanjahe and Berastagi in Tanah Karo area. While some towns around the Lake Toba offers freshwater fish dishes such as carp arsik. The Northern Sumatra capital of Medan is also a Batak cuisine hotspot where numerous of Lapo (Batak eating establishments) can be found, yet the city is also a culinary center of halal Malay Deli, as well as Indian Cuisine and  Chinese Indonesian cuisines. Outside its traditional lands in North Sumatra, Batak cuisine can also be found in Lapo in Jakarta, as well as most of the Indonesian major cities. Batak food is also widely present in Penang, Malaysia due to wide Batak migrant labour population found there."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"batak-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batak_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Dali ni horbo","dish/dali-ni-horbo"],["Itak gurgur","dish/itak-gurgur"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Bromelain","ingredient/bromelain"],["Papain","ingredient/papain"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Lampet","ingredient/lampet"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/4f/Batak_Cuisine_Saksang_and_Panggang_1.JPG/500px-Batak_Cuisine_Saksang_and_Panggang_1.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batak_Cuisine_Saksang_and_Panggang_1.JPG"}},"cuisine/cantabrian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cantabrian-cuisine","n":"Cantabrian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Spain","intro":[["Cantabrian cuisine is the cuisine from Cantabria, an autonomous community in northern Spain. It includes seafood from the Cantabrian Sea, salmon and trout from the upper basins of the rivers, vegetables and dairy products from the valleys, and veal and game from the Cantabrian Mountains."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cantabrian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantabrian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Cocido montañés","dish/cocido-montanes"],["Tuna pot","dish/tuna-pot"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Collard greens","ingredient/collard-greens"],["Common bean","ingredient/common-bean"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Tuna as food","ingredient/tuna-as-food"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f7/Highlander_stew.jpg/500px-Highlander_stew.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Jorge Sanz","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Highlander_stew.jpg"}},"ingredient/coconut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/coconut","n":"Coconut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 41 dishes across 23 cuisines","intro":[["The coconut (Cocos nucifera) is a member of the palm family (Arecaceae) and the only living species of the genus Cocos. The term \"coconut\" (or the archaic \"cocoanut\") can denote the whole coconut palm tree or the large hard fruit. Originally native to the Southeast Asia, New Guinea, and the Southwestern Pacific, they are ubiquitous in coastal tropical regions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-coconut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coconut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":23,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/